{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 4, :comments ["<b>Adonai, etc.</b> These are the words of the prophet." "<b>לִמוּדִים (taught ones).</b> It is an adjective of the divine name, and the witness is 'taught' in the verse (Jeremiah 12:3), 'to hear as those taught.' <b>וְלָעִת</b> is joined with 'to know' [and the word לָעַת is from the root עת]. <b>And the meaning of יָעֵף (wearies):</b> the heart of the student, when he learns difficult matters, grows weary and faint." "Now behold, <b>לָעִת:</b> to appoint a time, or it is a foreign word that has no cognate [literally: no evil to it, i.e., no related root in Hebrew]." "And the meaning of <b>יָעִיר etc.:</b> that HaShem awakens me morning by morning, and the interpretation of 'He awakens me' is that He awakens my ear so that I hear like the taught ones, and behold, I am a student of HaShem."], :book :isaiah, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 50, :notes {1 "The lemma למודים is left in Hebrew script per discipline; the gloss '(taught ones)' added because the grammatical argument requires identifying the form."}, :source-witnesses [["Ibn Ezra on Isaiah, by M. Friedlander; Society of Hebrew Literature, London 1877" "https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH001338443"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:58:56.205792297Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}